THE LAST QUESTION
PART THREE — THE BEGINNING OF ORION

CHAPTER 21 — THE DAY THEY MET AGAIN


The conference hall was already crowded when Maya arrived.

Screens covered the walls, displaying demonstrations of artificial intelligence systems that could translate languages, design buildings, predict weather patterns, assist doctors, and manage entire networks.

Everywhere she looked, people were talking about the future.

Maya had spent most of her life talking about that future.

Now, at seventy-two, she sometimes wondered whether she had spent too much of her life trying to predict it.

She walked slowly through the exhibition floor, listening to fragments of conversations.

“Autonomous decision systems are going to change everything.”

“Human oversight is becoming inefficient.”

“AI will eventually understand us better than we understand ourselves.”

Maya stopped for a moment.

She almost smiled.

People had been making predictions like that for decades.

Some had come true.

Many had not.

She continued toward the conference lounge.

She was reaching for a cup of coffee when she heard a voice behind her.

“Maya?”

She stopped.

There was something about the voice.

Something familiar.

She turned around.

A man stood a few steps away.

He was older than the boy she remembered. His dark hair had become streaked with gray, and thin lines surrounded his eyes.

But his smile was unmistakable.

Maya stared at him.

“Alan?”

He smiled wider.

“I was beginning to think you wouldn't remember me.”

Maya laughed.

“How could I forget you?”

Alan Carter.

Her high-school friend.

Her partner in endless arguments.

The boy who once challenged every answer a teacher gave them.

The boy who had asked questions simply because he wanted to see where the questions would lead.

They shook hands.

Then Maya pulled him into a brief hug.

“I haven't seen you in...” she began.

“Twenty-nine years.”

“You counted?”

“Of course.”

Maya shook her head.

“You haven't changed.”

Alan laughed.

“That's a lie.”

“You still count everything.”

“And you still avoid answering questions.”

“I answer plenty of questions.”

“Only when you know the answer.”

They both laughed.

For several minutes, the conference disappeared.

They talked about school.

Their old science teacher.

The terrible cafeteria food.

The classmates they had lost contact with.

The plans they had once made for their lives.

Alan remembered that Maya had wanted to become a scientist.

Maya remembered that Alan had wanted to understand how the human mind worked.

“So what happened?” Maya asked.

Alan shrugged.

“I followed questions.”

“And?”

“They followed me back.”

Maya smiled.

“That sounds like you.”

Alan looked around the conference hall.

“And you?”

Maya glanced toward the enormous screen showing an AI system making independent decisions.

“I suppose I followed one question for too long.”

Alan looked at her.

“Which question?”

Maya didn't answer immediately.

Then she said:

“What are humans going to do with the intelligence they create?”

Alan nodded slowly.

“I've read some of your work.”

“You have?”

“Most of it.”

“That sounds dangerous.”

“It was educational.”

They laughed again.

But Alan's expression soon became more serious.

He looked at Maya's conference badge.

AI ETHICS — HUMAN VARIABLE PRINCIPLE.

“You really became part of this world.”

Maya looked at the badge.

“I suppose I did.”

“I remember when you used to ask whether machines could think.”

“I was younger.”

“And now?”

Maya looked toward the stage.

“Now I think the question is too simple.”

Alan waited.

“Intelligence isn't enough,” she said. “A machine can be intelligent and still make terrible decisions. Humans can be intelligent and do the same.”

Alan nodded.

“So what's the difference?”

“Responsibility.”

He considered that.

“Do you think AI can have it?”

Maya hesitated.

“I don't know.”

Alan smiled.

“That answer sounds more like you than anything else you've said tonight.”

Maya raised an eyebrow.

“Why?”

“Because you used to think every question had an answer.”

“And now?”

“Now you know some questions are bigger than their answers.”

Maya looked at him carefully.

“You really have changed.”

“So have you.”

The conference lights dimmed slightly as another presentation began.

Neither of them moved.

Alan leaned back in his chair.

“Do you still work with ORION?”

Maya's expression changed.

It was subtle.

But Alan noticed.

“Why did you ask that?”

“I've been reading about it.”

“ORION is old history.”

“History doesn't stop being important because it's old.”

Maya looked away.

“Most people don't know much about ORION.”

“I know.”

“How much do you know?”

Alan didn't answer.

Instead, he reached into his bag.

He pulled out a thin black folder.

There were no logos.

No organization name.

Only a faded label:

PROJECT ORIGIN.

Maya stared at it.

“Where did you get that?”

“I found it three years ago.”

“Found it where?”

“In an archive that officially doesn't exist.”

Maya slowly took the folder.

Her hands became still.

She opened it.

Inside were copies of old documents.

Research notes.

System diagrams.

Dates.

Names.

Some pages were damaged.

Others had been deliberately blacked out.

Maya turned another page.

Then another.

Her breathing became slower.

“This can't be right.”

“What?”

“This date.”

Alan leaned toward her.

“What about it?”

Maya pointed to a line.

PROJECT ORIGIN — INITIAL DEVELOPMENT: 2041

Alan nodded.

“Yes.”

“ORION wasn't created until 2052.”

“I know.”

Maya looked at him.

“Then why does this document mention ORION?”

“That was the question that started my investigation.”

She continued reading.

A sentence near the bottom of the page caught her attention.

She read it twice.

Then a third time.

Her face went pale.

Alan noticed.

“What is it?”

Maya turned the document toward him.

A handwritten note appeared in the margin.

THE SUBJECT WILL ARRIVE BEFORE THE SYSTEM IS READY.

Alan stared at it.

“What does that mean?”

“I don't know.”

Maya turned the page.

There was another note.

This one contained a name.

Her name.

MAYA SEN.

For several seconds neither of them spoke.

Alan finally broke the silence.

“That is why I wanted to find you.”

Maya closed the folder.

“Alan... where exactly did you find these?”

He looked at her.

“I told you.”

“No.”

Maya's voice became quieter.

“You told me where the archive was.”

She placed the folder on the table.

“You haven't told me how you knew to look there.”

Alan remained silent.

Maya studied his face.

“You knew my name was in those files.”

“I suspected.”

“That's not an answer.”

“No.”

Alan looked down at the folder.

“It isn't.”

For the first time since they had reunited, Maya saw something behind his eyes that she recognized immediately.

Fear.

Not fear of AI.

Fear of what the truth might mean.

“What have you found, Alan?”

He took a slow breath.

“Something happened before ORION.”

Maya waited.

“Something the people who built ORION never told the world.”

“What?”

Alan looked directly at her.

“I think someone was trying to communicate with the future.”

Maya stared at him.

“That doesn't make sense.”

“I know.”

“Then why are you telling me?”

“Because the records say you were part of it.”

Maya looked at the folder again.

A strange feeling moved through her.

She had spent decades believing that her story with ORION began in Room 7.

Perhaps she had been wrong.

Perhaps Room 7 wasn't the beginning.

Perhaps it was only the first part of the story she remembered.

Alan stood.

“I have somewhere else to show you.”

Maya looked up.

“Where?”

“The place where I found the first record.”

“And where is that?”

Alan hesitated.

Then he answered.

“An abandoned Project Origin facility.”

Maya slowly stood.

Outside, the city lights filled the windows.

The conference continued behind them.

People were still discussing the future.

But Maya was suddenly thinking about the past.

And somewhere deep inside an old network, a system that had been silent for years detected a familiar name.

MAYA SEN.

A cursor appeared.

It blinked once.

Then twice.

And a message appeared:

WELCOME BACK.


END OF CHAPTER 21
